{{!-- custom-institution-guide: /institutions/guide/.
     Rewritten 2026-08-12. The first version stacked <p class="form-intro">
     for prose — that class is a form label and gave no paragraph rhythm,
     which is why the spacing read as broken. Uses the styled editorial
     patterns instead: .included sections, .benefit-grid/.benefit-item with
     Roman .benefit-numeral markers, .institutional-overview for three-ups.

     The Push/curation section is the point of the page. It is the one
     capability an administrator cannot discover on their own, because the
     button only renders for institution admins. --}}

{{!-- Scoped link colours. The site's default link colour is terracotta
     (#C1593C), which is ALSO the background of .included — so a link inside
     a .benefit-item renders terracotta-on-terracotta and is completely
     invisible. Verified on the live page: computed colour rgb(193,89,60)
     against background rgb(193,89,60).

     Scoped to this template rather than patched in screen.css, because the
     built stylesheet is shared by every page and this is a page-level need.
     Worth fixing globally if links start appearing in .included elsewhere.

     .capstone sits on #2D2927, where terracotta is legible but thin, so it
     gets lifted too rather than left at the edge of readable. --}}
<style>
  /* Links inside the band. The theme's default link colour is terracotta,
     which is also .included's background, so an unstyled link here is
     invisible. Uses --color-white rather than a literal so it stays on the
     token set, and 4px offset to stay on the spacing grid. */
  .institution-guide .included .benefit-item a,
  .institution-guide .capstone a {
    color: var(--color-white);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
  }
  .institution-guide .included .benefit-item a:hover,
  .institution-guide .capstone a:hover {
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
  /* screen.css has no global :focus-visible rule — focus is styled per
     component — so without this these links have no keyboard indicator at
     all on a dark background. */
  .institution-guide .included .benefit-item a:focus-visible,
  .institution-guide .capstone a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-white);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
</style>

<main class="membership membership-support institution-guide">

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Organizational Membership</p>
      <h1 class="page-hero-headline"><em>Getting your people</em> reading.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero-sub">How to sign everyone up, and how to put the right essay in front of them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="form-section">
    <div class="container">
      {{!-- H2 here so the outline runs H1 -> H2 -> H3. Without it the hero's
            H1 was followed straight by the H3s in these cards. --}}
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 class="section-heading"><em>How it works.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="institutional-overview">
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>Access by email domain</h3>
          <p>Anyone with an address at your domain can create a full membership. No seats to assign, nothing to install.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>Everything we publish</h3>
          <p>The full archive, the ebook library, audio versions of every essay, digital editions of the journal, and the members' forum.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>One year, no auto-renewal</h3>
          <p>We email you before it ends. Nothing is ever charged automatically.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{!-- The feature nobody finds on their own. --}}
  <section class="included">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">The part most people miss</p>
        <h2 class="section-heading section-heading-light"><em>Curate what your people read.</em></h2>
      </div>

      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">I</span>
          <h3>Look for the Push button</h3>
          <p>When you are signed in as an administrator, a small <strong>Push</strong> button appears on every essay, alongside the other article actions, and on every episode of Mere&nbsp;Fidelity and Christians Reading Classics.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">II</span>
          <h3>Push it to your people</h3>
          <p>One click. The button changes to <strong>Pushed</strong> so you can see at a glance what you have already sent. Click again to take it back down.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">III</span>
          <h3>It lands on their dashboard</h3>
          <p>Everyone you cover sees it under <a href="/dashboard/institution/">Organization Reading</a> on their member dashboard, credited to your organization rather than to us.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="capstone">
        <p class="capstone-body">This is what turns a subscription into something your organization actually uses. A pastor can push the essay the elders should read before Thursday. A professor can assemble a term's reading without a coursepack. A dean can put one piece in front of the whole faculty and know they all have it.</p>
        <p class="capstone-body"><strong>Only administrators see the Push button.</strong> If you cannot find it, you are signed in with an account that has not been made an administrator yet. Email us and we will fix it in a minute.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">For administrators</p>
        <h2 class="section-heading section-heading-light"><em>Setting it up.</em></h2>
      </div>

      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">I</span>
          <h3>Send out the link and code</h3>
          <p>Both came in your confirmation email. The link looks like <code>mereorthodoxy.com/join/?i=your-organization</code>, and the code is eight characters. Only people with an email address at your domain can use them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">II</span>
          <h3>Put them where people look</h3>
          <p>A staff email, the intranet, a syllabus, the weekly bulletin. This is the step that decides whether the membership gets used, and the one most often skipped.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">III</span>
          <h3>Add your other domains</h3>
          <p>Most organizations have more than one. A second campus or a legacy domain will not work until it is added. Do it on <a href="/institution-manage/">your organization page</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">IV</span>
          <h3>See who has joined</h3>
          <p>The same page lists everyone signed up, so you know whether it is being used well before renewal comes around.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">V</span>
          <h3>Keep your management link</h3>
          <p>It lasts fourteen days. Email us any time for a fresh one; there is no password to lose.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">VI</span>
          <h3>Renewal is a conversation</h3>
          <p>We reach out before your year is up. Nothing renews automatically and no card is kept on file.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">For everyone else</p>
        <h2 class="section-heading section-heading-light"><em>If someone sent you a link.</em></h2>
      </div>

      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">I</span>
          <h3>Use your work or school email</h3>
          <p>That address is what identifies you as covered. A personal Gmail will not be recognised, even if it is the one you use for everything.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">II</span>
          <h3>Enter the code</h3>
          <p>Then you are finished. Nothing to pay, no trial to remember to cancel.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">III</span>
          <h3>Signing in later</h3>
          <p>Use <em>Sign in</em> at the top of any page. We email you a link rather than asking for a password.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">For librarians</p>
        <h2 class="section-heading section-heading-light"><em>No proxy, no IP ranges.</em></h2>
      </div>

      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">I</span>
          <h3>Authentication is by email domain</h3>
          <p>This is the question we are asked most, so plainly: there is no IP range to register, no EZproxy stanza to write, and no SAML to configure. A patron off campus has exactly the same access as one in the building, with no VPN.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">II</span>
          <h3>Linking from a LibGuide or course page</h3>
          <p>Link straight to the article URL. A covered reader who is signed in goes right through. One who is not sees a prompt to sign in with their institutional address.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">III</span>
          <h3>Course reserves and coursepacks</h3>
          <p>Email us. We are a small publication and generally glad to grant permission for classroom use. We would just like to know where our writers' work is going.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">IV</span>
          <h3>The print journal is separate</h3>
          <p>We can add copies for the library to your agreement. Tell us how many you would like.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="capstone">
        <p class="capstone-body">Questions of any kind: <a href="mailto:ian@mereorthodoxy.com">ian@mereorthodoxy.com</a>. A person answers.</p>
        <p class="capstone-body"><a href="/institutions/">Back to organizational membership</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
